    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We can use the distributive property to solve this problem. First, we multiply the terms individually. Then, we combine the results with subtraction. To organize the result, we arrange the expression in standard form - decreasing degree form. That is, the polynomial is ordered from highest degree to lowest. We add parentheses for clarity where needed.\n\nMultiplying each term in $(2x+3)$ by each term in $(x+5)$ gives us:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n(2x+3)(x+5) & = (2x)(x) + (2x)(5) + (3)(x) + (3)(5) \\\\\n& = 2x^2 + 10x + 3x + 15 \\\\\n& = 2x^2 + 13x + 15\n\\end{align*}\n\nSo, the expanded form of $(2x+3)(x+5)$ is $\\boxed{2x^2 + 13x + 15}$.",
